quiz:
  - q: 'A fund''s NAV was \(\$120\) at its peak, fell to \(\$96\), and is now \(\$130\). With a \(20\%\) performance fee and a high-water mark, what fee is due per share?'
    opts: ['\(\$2\)', '\(\$6.80\)', '\(\$0\)']
    right: 0
    why: 'The fee only applies above the old peak of \(\$120\): \(20\% \times (\$130 - \$120) = \$2\). The climb back from \(\$96\) to \(\$120\) earns nothing.'
  - q: 'A fund has a \(5\%\) hurdle and returns \(15\%\). How does a soft hurdle differ from a hard hurdle?'
    opts: ['A soft hurdle charges the fee only on the \(10\%\) above the hurdle', 'There''s no difference once the hurdle is beaten', 'A soft hurdle charges the fee on the whole \(15\%\), because the hurdle was beaten']
    right: 2
    why: 'Hard: the fee is on returns above the hurdle (\(10\%\)). Soft: the hurdle is just a gate; once it''s cleared, the fee is on everything from zero (\(15\%\)). Soft is better for the manager.'
  - q: 'A new fund''s running costs are \(\$3\) million a year and it charges a \(1.5\%\) management fee. What''s its breakeven size?'
    opts: ['\(\$45\) million', '\(\$200\) million', '\(\$2\) billion']
    right: 1
    why: '\(\$3\text{m} \div 0.015 = \$200\) million. In practice funds aim higher, \(\$250\) to \(\$500\) million, to pay good people and build reserves for lean years.'

