{% sec 1, "Two Ways to Get Paid", "What does a hedge fund actually charge?", "Two fees" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Think of a personal trainer who charges you two ways: a monthly fee whether or not you get fitter, and a bonus if you hit your target. The monthly fee keeps the gym open. The bonus is meant to make them care about results.</p>
    <p>A hedge fund charges the same two ways:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The <strong>{% gl "management fee" %}management fee{% endgl %}</strong> is a percentage of the money in the fund, charged every year whatever happens. It's usually worked out monthly or quarterly. A \(\$1\) billion fund charging \(1.5\%\) earns \(\$15\) million a year before it makes a single trade. It's meant to pay for salaries, offices, technology, data, lawyers and compliance.</li>
      <li>The <strong>{% gl "performance fee" %}performance fee{% endgl %}</strong> is a share of the profits, usually \(15\) to \(20\%\). It's what's supposed to line the manager's interests up with the investors'. In a partnership it's normally written as an "allocation" of the fund's income rather than a fee, which can give the manager better tax treatment on long-term gains.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The classic deal is <strong>{% gl "2 and 20" %}"2 and 20"{% endgl %}</strong>: \(2\%\) a year plus \(20\%\) of the profits. Alfred Winslow Jones, who started it all, actually charged only the \(20\%\), with no management fee. Today the average is about \(1.4\%\) and \(17\%\), and the biggest investors negotiate less.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Typical</th><th>Charged on</th><th>A \(\$500\) million fund returning \(15\%\)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Management fee</td><td>\(1.5\)–\(2\%\)</td><td>The money in the fund, every year</td><td>\(2\% \times \$500\text{m} = \$10\text{m}\)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Performance fee</td><td>\(15\)–\(20\%\)</td><td>Profits above the high-water mark</td><td>\(20\% \times (\$75\text{m} - \$10\text{m}) = \$13\text{m}\)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hurdle</td><td>\(0\)–\(8\%\)</td><td>A return the fund must beat first</td><td>With a \(6\%\) hard hurdle: \(20\% \times (\$65\text{m} - \$30\text{m}) = \$7\text{m}\)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>High-water mark</td><td>Almost always</td><td>No fee until the fund is back above its old peak</td><td>Nothing extra here: the fund is at a new peak</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Crystallisation</td><td>Yearly, sometimes quarterly</td><td>When the performance fee is locked in</td><td>Usually worked out on 31 December</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Clawback</td><td>Rare</td><td>Paying back earlier fees after later losses</td><td>Hard to enforce in practice</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Here the performance fee is worked out on the profit after the management fee, which is the usual way. Some documents calculate it before; it's one of the details investors check.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 2, "The High-Water Mark", "Why can't a manager get paid twice for the same gains?", "The high-water mark" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Imagine paying a salesperson a bonus every time the shop's monthly sales go up. In a bad month sales fall to half; the next month they recover to normal. Should you pay a bonus for "growth"? You've just got back where you were.</p>
    <p>The {% gl "high-water mark" %}high-water mark{% endgl %} (HWM) stops that. It's the highest value the fund has ever reached after fees. It moves up every time the fund hits a new peak, and never moves down. The manager only earns a performance fee on gains above it.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">How it works</span>
    <h3>A fund's NAV peaks at \(\$120\) a share, falls to \(\$96\), then recovers.</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Back to \(\$115\): no performance fee. The fund is still below its \(\$120\) mark.</li>
      <li>Up to \(\$130\): the fee is only on the \(\$10\) above \(\$120\). At \(20\%\), that's \(\$2\) a share.</li>
      <li>The new high-water mark is \(\$128\) (the \(\$130\) less the \(\$2\) fee).</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">The \(\$24\) climb from \(\$96\) back to \(\$120\) earns the manager nothing: that's the protection.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>Life under water</h3>
    <p>A fund that's far below its high-water mark is called "under water", and it's a hard place to be. The manager has to make back the whole loss before earning any performance fee, and lives on the management fee alone until then. A fund that falls \(30\%\) needs a \(43\%\) gain just to get back to its mark. That causes problems:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Talent leaves.</strong> Analysts paid from performance fees can earn more at a fund that isn't under water.</li>
      <li><strong>Funds close and relaunch.</strong> Some managers shut the fund and start a new one with a fresh mark rather than work for years for free. Investors notice.</li>
      <li><strong>Incentives bend.</strong> An under-water manager may take big risks to get back quickly, since a loss costs them little more; or turn very cautious to protect the management fee.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The same rule applies to a portfolio manager's own pay on a multi-manager platform. Life on a Desk has an interactive showing how it works over three years.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 3, "Hurdles, Crystallisation and Clawbacks", "What else decides how much of a good year the manager takes?", "Hurdles and the fine print" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>Hurdles: beat cash first</h3>
    <p>A {% gl "hurdle rate" %}hurdle{% endgl %} is a return the fund must beat before any performance fee is earned. The argument: an investor could earn interest in cash for free, so why pay \(20\%\) of a return they'd have got anyway? Hurdles are often set at the cash rate (for example SOFR) or a fixed \(4\) to \(8\%\). They come in two kinds:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A <strong>hard hurdle</strong> charges the fee only on returns <em>above</em> it. Hurdle \(5\%\), return \(15\%\): fee on \(10\%\).</li>
      <li>A <strong>soft hurdle</strong> is just a gate. Once the fund beats it, the fee is on the <em>whole</em> return from zero. Hurdle \(5\%\), return \(15\%\): fee on \(15\%\). Better for the manager.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Crystallisation: when the fee is locked in</h3>
    <p>During the year, the administrator accrues the performance fee as the fund rises and falls, but it's only {% gl "crystallisation" %}crystallised{% endgl %} (made final and paid) at set dates, usually 31 December. The more often it crystallises, the better for the manager. Picture a fund up \(15\%\) by June that ends the year flat: with yearly crystallisation there's no fee; with quarterly crystallisation, the manager has already banked fees on the first-half gains, and the high-water mark has moved up past where the fund ends.</p>
    <h3>Clawbacks: rare in hedge funds</h3>
    <p>A {% gl "clawback" %}clawback{% endgl %} makes the manager pay back past performance fees if later losses wipe out the gains. It sounds fair, but it's rare in hedge funds and hard to enforce: the fees have long since been paid out as bonuses. It's more common in private equity and property funds, where the big payments come at the end.</p>
  </div>
