{% sec 4, "What It Costs to Run a Fund", "How big does a fund need to be to pay its bills?", "Fund economics" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Performance fees are uncertain: some years there aren't any. So the first question for anyone starting a fund is how much money it needs to manage for the management fee alone to cover the costs. That's the <strong>breakeven size</strong>:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Breakeven assets under management</span>\[ \text{breakeven AUM} = \frac{\text{yearly running costs}}{\text{management fee rate}} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>With \(\$3\) million of costs and a \(1.5\%\) fee, that's \(\$200\) million. But breakeven isn't enough: the fee also has to pay the founders, attract good analysts, and build a reserve for years with no performance fee. In practice, most people in the industry think a new fund needs \(\$250\) to \(\$500\) million to be viable, and the bar keeps rising as technology, data, compliance and people get more expensive. Getting there usually means a <strong>seeder</strong> (a firm that puts in \(\$50\) to \(\$200\) million in return for a share of the fund's revenue or ownership), an <strong>anchor investor</strong> (a big first investor who gets lower fees in return), or years of building from the founders' own money and wealthy contacts. The Raising Capital note covers how.</p>
    <h3>The magic of scale</h3>
    <p>Once a fund is past breakeven, it gets very profitable very fast. Managing \(\$1\) billion doesn't cost five times as much as managing \(\$200\) million: it's mostly the same team, systems and office. But it brings in five times the management fee. That's {% gl "operating leverage" %}operating leverage{% endgl %}, and it's why successful hedge funds are such good businesses, and why the first few years are so hard.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
